## [Building your tagging strategy](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/tagging-best-practices/building-your-tagging-strategy.md)

- [Defining needs and use cases](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/tagging-best-practices/defining-needs-and-use-cases.md): Start building your strategy by engaging with stakeholders who have a fundamental underlying need to consume metadata.
- [Defining and publishing a tagging schema](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/tagging-best-practices/defining-and-publishing-a-tagging-schema.md): Employ a consistent approach in tagging your AWS resources, both for mandatory and optional tags.
- [AWS Organizations – Tag policies](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/tagging-best-practices/aws-organizations-tag-policies.md): Policies in AWS Organizations allow you to apply additional types of governance to AWS accounts in your organization.
- [Implementing and enforcing tagging](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/tagging-best-practices/implementing-and-enforcing-tagging.md): In this section, we’ll introduce you to the tools available for the following resource management strategies: manual, infrastructure as code (IaC), and continuous integration/continuous delivery (CI/CD).
- [Measuring tagging effectiveness and driving improvements](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/tagging-best-practices/measuring-tagging-effectiveness-and-driving-improvements.md): After you have implemented a tagging strategy, it's important to measure its effectiveness against the target use cases.


## [Tagging use cases](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/tagging-best-practices/tagging-use-cases.md)

### [Tags for cost allocation and financial management](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/tagging-best-practices/tags-for-cost-allocation-and-financial-management.md)

Tags for cost allocation and financial management

- [Cost allocation tags](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/tagging-best-practices/cost-allocation-tags.md): Cost allocation refers to the assignment or distribution of incurred costs to the users or beneficiaries of those costs following a defined process.
- [Building a cost allocation strategy](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/tagging-best-practices/building-a-cost-allocation-strategy.md): Defining and implementing a cost allocation model.

### [Tags for operations and support](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/tagging-best-practices/tags-for-operations-and-support.md)

An AWS environment will have multiple accounts, resources, and workloads with differing operational requirements.

- [Automated infrastructure activities](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/tagging-best-practices/automated-infrastructure-activities.md): Tags can be used in a wide range of automation activities when managing infrastructure.
- [Workload lifecycle](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/tagging-best-practices/workload-lifecycle.md): Review accuracy of supporting operational data.
- [Incident management](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/tagging-best-practices/incident-management.md): Tags can play a vital part in all phases of incident management starting from incident logging, prioritization, investigation, communication, resolution to closure.
- [Patching](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/tagging-best-practices/patching.md): Organizations can automate their patching strategy for mutable compute environments and keep mutable instances in-line with the defined patch baseline of that application environment by using AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager and AWS Lambda.
- [Operational observability](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/tagging-best-practices/operational-observability.md): Observability is required to gain actionable insights into the performance of your environments and help you to detect and investigate problems.

### [Tags for data security, risk management, and access control](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/tagging-best-practices/tags-for-data-security-risk-management-and-access-control.md)

Organizations have varying needs and obligations to meet regarding the appropriate handling of data storage and processing.

- [Data security and risk management](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/tagging-best-practices/data-security-and-risk-management.md): Within an AWS environment, you will probably have accounts with differing compliance and security requirements.

### [Tags for identity management and access control](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/tagging-best-practices/tags-for-identity-management-and-access-control.md)

When managing access control across an AWS environment with AWS IAM Identity Center, tags can enable several patterns for scaling.

- [ABAC for individual resources](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/tagging-best-practices/abac-for-individual-resources.md): IAM Identity Center users and IAM roles support attribute-based access control (ABAC), which allows you to define access to operations and resources based on tags.


